from review import card,save,DATE,DIRECTORY,write
i='TCS-5180';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Question 1 is answered in Theorems 2–8 of the same paper. Section 2 defines one-sided approximation, the dimensions and randomized ETH; Section 7 separates the still-open task of approximately optimal online learning.'
refs += [dict(id='preprint',title='Improved Inapproximability of VC Dimension and Littlestone’s Dimension via (Unbalanced) Biclique — author preprint',authors='Pasin Manurangsi',year=2022,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.01443',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2211.01443v1',locator='Version 1, 2 November 2022. Abstract and version history checked; complete mathematical definitions and results read in the 18-page published ITCS 2023 paper.')]
save(i,dict(formal=r'Historical Question 1: can either VC dimension or Littlestone dimension of an explicitly listed finite Boolean concept class be approximated within some fixed constant factor in polynomial time? Under randomized ETH, the answer is negative for each dimension, already by Theorem 2 of the cited paper. “Either” allows choosing one dimension once; it does not require one algorithm to estimate both simultaneously.',definitions=r'''The input lists a finite domain \(X\) and a nonempty finite class \(\mathcal C\) of Boolean functions on \(X\), by its full binary incidence matrix \(M_{x,c}=c(x)\). The target is not given by a succinct circuit or by a sampling oracle. Domain size and number of listed concepts both vary with the input; duplicate concept columns may be removed. Let \(L\) denote the total explicit encoding length, with \(L\geq2\) for asymptotic notation.

The VC dimension is the largest integer \(d\geq0\) for which some set \(S\subseteq X\) of size \(d\) is shattered: every function \(S\to\{0,1\}\) occurs as the restriction of a concept in \(\mathcal C\).

For Littlestone dimension, consider a deterministic online learner with no computational restriction. On each round it receives \(x_{t}\in X\), predicts a bit, then learns the true bit \(y_{t}\). The finite sequence is realizable when a single concept \(c\in\mathcal C\) satisfies \(c(x_{t})=y_{t}\) on every round. The learner's mistake bound is the maximum number of wrong predictions over all realizable sequences of any finite length. Littlestone dimension is the minimum such bound over all learners.

Equivalently, Littlestone dimension is the greatest depth of a complete binary tree whose internal nodes are labeled by elements of \(X\), whose outgoing edges have labels \(0,1\), and for which every root-to-leaf sequence of examples and edge labels is realized by some concept. The concept may differ between paths. These are combinatorial definitions of the number being estimated; constructing an approximately optimal online learner is a separate algorithmic problem.

Fix one of the two dimensions and write its value as \(D\). A constant-factor approximation means that there is a fixed \(r\geq1\), independent of the matrix dimensions and input, and a uniform polynomial-time algorithm outputting a binary-encoded rational \(\widehat D\) with
\[D/r\leq\widehat D\leq D.\]
For a randomized algorithm, this must hold with probability at least \(2/3\) on every input. Time is polynomial in \(L\) in the worst case. No shattered set or online strategy must accompany the numerical estimate. When \(D=0\), successful approximation requires output zero.

Randomized ETH, the assumption used here, asserts that no randomized \(2^{o(N)}\)-time algorithm decides satisfiability of all \(N\)-variable 3CNF formulas with bounded error. Polynomial dependence on the explicit formula length is allowed; repetitions can be removed so that its size is polynomial in \(N\). The paper's Section 2.2 expressly treats reductions and the associated hypothesis as randomized. A deterministic-only version of ETH is not silently substituted.

The conditional negative result rules out every fixed factor \(r\), including large constants, for each of the dimensions separately. It is not an unconditional lower bound, and it is not merely hardness of finding the exact integer value.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The source already proves the negative answer under randomized ETH. A positive constant-factor polynomial-time algorithm for either dimension would contradict that assumption. The historical question is therefore recorded as conditionally resolved rather than as an unreviewed open challenge; no unconditional nonexistence assertion is made.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The extracted question is answered immediately afterward',text='Question 1 appears in the introduction to motivate the paper. Theorem 2 rules out constant-factor approximation under randomized ETH, and other theorems establish stronger guarantees under stronger or alternative hypotheses.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The numerical objective is small but still difficult',text=r'For a nonempty class with \(m\) distinct concepts, both dimensions are at most \(\lfloor\log_{2}m\rfloor\). Zero-dimensional cases can be recognized directly, and outputting one on the remaining cases gives an \(O(\log L)\)-factor estimate. Thus a coarse logarithmic approximation does not supply an arbitrary fixed constant factor.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The paper proves more than constant-factor hardness',text=r'Theorem 2 excludes polynomial-time approximation within \(o(\log L/(\log\log L)^{\xi})\) for some constant \(\xi>0\), under randomized ETH. Under the paper’s randomized Gap-ETH or Strongish Planted Clique Hypothesis, Theorems 3 and 4 strengthen this to \(o(\log L)\). Those extra hypotheses are not needed for the constant-factor negative answer.',citation='primary'),dict(title='An adjacent online-learning question remains distinct',text='The conclusion notes that computing a near-optimal mistake-bound number is not known to be equivalent to constructing an efficient online learner with a near-optimal mistake bound. This unresolved approximate-strategy question is not substituted for the extracted numerical approximation task.',citation='primary')],why='Shows a computational obstruction to evaluating basic combinatorial measures of learning difficulty, even when the entire class is explicitly available. Constant-factor hardness matters because these measures are often used only up to such factors when assessing statistical or online learning requirements.',progress=[dict(date='2022-11-02',text='The author preprint announces approximation hardness for explicitly listed concept classes.',citation='preprint'),dict(date='2023-02-01',text='The published paper answers its introductory constant-factor question negatively under randomized ETH and proves stronger conditional approximation bounds.',citation='primary')],references=refs,status='resolved',status_note='Conditionally resolved in the original source. Read Question 1, Theorems 2–8, Sections 2.1–2.3 and the conclusion; checked the author preprint history and searched subsequent work through 12 September 2026. Randomized ETH suffices for the stated negative answer and is explicitly retained. The source’s separate approximate-online-learner question is not the same problem.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Recovered the full explicit-matrix problem, one-sided multiplicative convention and randomized resource assumption. Defined both dimensions and corrected the stale open status using the same source’s answer.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A substantive hardness classification for evaluating the learning complexity of an explicitly represented class. Existing score retained as a historical assessment.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The entire Boolean concept class is supplied as a binary matrix.','The historical question asks for a fixed-factor polynomial-time estimate of either VC or Littlestone dimension.','The approximation is multiplicative, with zero handled exactly.','The cited paper already rules this out for each dimension under randomized ETH.','Constructing an efficient online learner with a near-optimal mistake bound is a separate question.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary'])),notes=['Defined the explicit matrix input and both combinatorial dimensions.','Specified constant-factor numerical output, zero cases and randomized success.','Read the original paper’s answer and retained its randomized ETH hypothesis.','Marked the historical task conditionally resolved without replacing it by the separate online strategy problem.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
p=DIRECTORY/'queue.json';q=json.loads(p.read_text());_,c=card(i)
if not any(r['id']==i for r in q['dispositions']):q['dispositions'].append(dict(id=i,title=c['title'],state='resolved',reason=c['status_note'],card='data/cards/'+i+'.json'))
